    title: 'Everyone Has a Story', skill: 'speaking',
    goal: 'I can explain a reporting purpose and establish boundaries around voluntary participation.',
    intro: 'After ten years in journalism, Emi Sato leaves her newspaper to launch The Things We Don’t Say. At Daisuke’s café in Aokawa, she discusses loneliness, poverty, family expectations and discrimination with friends. Producer Leila asks how people might share their experiences. Hana argues for a setting where speaking is a choice. Emi wants meaningful reporting without using personal struggles merely to attract attention.',
    warmup: 'What could a reporting team clarify before inviting someone to discuss a sensitive experience?',
    expressions: [
      ['bring an issue into the open', 'means to make a difficult or overlooked subject available for discussion.', 'Emi wants to <strong>bring overlooked issues into the open</strong>.'],
      ['participate on your own terms', 'means to take part with choices and boundaries that you can understand and express.', 'Guests should be able to <strong>participate on their own terms</strong>.'],
      ['draw an ethical boundary', 'means to establish a limit based on what you believe is responsible or fair.', 'Hana helps the team <strong>draw an ethical boundary</strong> around pressure to disclose.'],
      ['exploit someone’s experience', 'means to use what happened to a person mainly for your own advantage, without sufficient regard for them.', 'Emi does not want to <strong>exploit someone’s experience</strong> for attention.'],
    ],
    discussion: 'II. You are Emi. Your tutor is Leila. Explain the podcast’s purpose and how you want to invite participation.', followup: 'How could the team create an opportunity to speak without treating disclosure as something it is owed?',
    tips: [
      { rule: 'State the purpose and the participation boundary together.', examples: ['We want to discuss overlooked issues, while leaving people free to decline.', 'An invitation should not become pressure to reveal a personal experience.'] },
      { rule: 'Use rather than to clarify the distinction between two approaches.', examples: ['We should create a choice rather than persuade someone to disclose.', 'The aim is understanding rather than attention at a guest’s expense.'] },
    ],
    apply: { situation: 'Leila and Emi discuss the new podcast at Daisuke’s café after Hana raises the question of choice.', roles: ['Leila', 'Emi'], dialogue: [
      ['Leila', 'How do you want this podcast to bring difficult issues into the open?'], ['Emi', 'By listening to residents of Aokawa about pressures that often stay hidden behind everyday appearances.'],
      ['Leila', 'Loneliness and family expectations could be valuable subjects. How do we convince people to share?'], ['Emi', 'Hana’s point matters: we should invite them rather than convince them. They need room to decide.'],
      ['Leila', 'What would participating on their own terms mean in practice?'], ['Emi', 'We can explain our purpose, listen to their boundaries and accept that some experiences may stay private.'],
      ['Leila', 'So an interesting story does not automatically belong in the programme?'], ['Emi', 'Exactly. I want to draw an ethical boundary between reporting sensitively and exploiting someone’s experience for attention.'],
      ['Leila', 'Daisuke says he can sponsor us with coffee rather than a large production budget.'], ['Emi', 'Then we have a modest beginning and a responsibility to put the people behind the stories first.'],
    ], questions: [['Why is Emi starting the podcast?', 'To discuss social issues and pressures people often hesitate to talk about.'], ['How does Hana reshape the participation question?', 'Create conditions for a voluntary choice rather than convince people to disclose.'], ['What does Daisuke offer?', 'Coffee as a light-hearted form of sponsorship, not a substantial funding agreement.']] },
    choose: [['Emi wants to bring the issue (into / under) the open.', 'into'], ['Guests can participate on their own (terms / termed).', 'terms'], ['The team needs to draw an ethical (boundary / bounded).', 'boundary'], ['They should not exploit someone’s (experience / experienced).', 'experience'], ['We can invite people rather than (pressure / pressured) them.', 'pressure'], ['A compelling story does not automatically (justify / justifies) publication.', 'justify']],
    choices: [['Which approach follows Hana’s suggestion?', 'Explain the invitation and leave participation optional.', 'Treat a refusal as an obstacle to overcome.', 'A'], ['What can the coffee joke establish?', 'The podcast has a major commercial funding deal.', 'Daisuke offers modest support and humour.', 'B'], ['What is Emi’s reporting aim?', 'Understanding overlooked experiences without exploiting them.', 'Maximising attention regardless of a guest’s boundaries.', 'A'], ['What remains ahead?', 'Emi’s departure from the newspaper.', 'The interviews and any decision about what to publish.', 'B']],
    mission: { situation: 'You are Emi. Your tutor is Leila. You are preparing The Things We Don’t Say in Aokawa. Hana has emphasised voluntary participation, and Daisuke has offered coffee as a small gesture of support.', task: 'Explain the reporting purpose, describe a respectful invitation and identify a boundary the team should accept.', roles: ['Leila', 'Emi'], lines: ['Emi, what should a listener understand about the purpose of our podcast?', 'How would you invite a resident without making them feel obliged to disclose?', 'What should we do if someone wants to keep part of a story private?', 'How would you distinguish a compelling account from permission to publish it?', 'What principle should guide us when attention and a guest’s wishes pull in different directions?'], evidence: 'New independent cast, fictional Aokawa neighbourhood in Tokyo. Emi has ten years of journalism experience and has left a major newspaper. Riku is community organiser, Leila podcast producer, Daisuke café owner; Hana’s profession is unspecified. No real institution, substantial sponsor contract, completed interview or publication yet. Discuss responsible reporting through assigned roles without soliciting personal disclosures from learner.', useful: ['The purpose is to ...', 'We would invite people to ..., while ...', 'They should be free to ...', 'An interesting story does not necessarily ...', 'Our boundary would be ...'] },
    topics: [['REPORTING PURPOSE', ['What distinguishes public interest from curiosity about someone’s private life?', 'How could a programme explain its purpose clearly?', 'What might careful listening add to a social-issues discussion?']], ['VOLUNTARY PARTICIPATION', ['How can an invitation leave room for refusal?', 'Why might a participant’s boundaries differ from a producer’s preferences?', 'What could a team clarify before recording?']], ['RESPONSIBILITY', ['How might good intentions still produce pressure?', 'What would make a reporting choice fair to the person involved?', 'How could a team discuss limits before a deadline creates urgency?']]],
    trivia: 'An “ethical boundary” identifies a limit in how people choose to act. In this story, the team connects that limit with voluntary participation. Describing the boundary clearly helps them discuss a difficult decision before they face a publication deadline.', triviaQuestion: ['Does having an important subject remove the need to respect a guest’s choice?', 'No. The team treats participation as voluntary.'],
